<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="Configuration" id="configuration">
    <p>
        <code>KrpcConfig</code> is a class used to configure <code>KrpcClient</code> and <code>KrpcServer</code>
        (not to be confused with <code>RpcClient</code> and <code>RpcServer</code>).
    </p>
    <p>
        It has two children: <code>KrpcConfig.Client</code> and <code>KrpcConfig.Server</code>.
        <code>Client</code> and <code>Server</code> may have shared properties as well as distinct ones.
        To create instances of these configurations, DSL builders are provided:
    </p>
    <list>
        <li>
            <code>rpcClientConfig</code>
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>rpcServerConfig</code>
        </li>
    </list>

    <code-block lang="kotlin">
        val config: KrpcConfig.Client = rpcClientConfig { // same for KrpcConfig.Server with rpcServerConfig
            // configuration here
        }
    </code-block>
    <p>
        The following configuration options are available:
    </p>
    <chapter id="serialization-dsl">
        <title>
            <code>serialization</code> DSL
        </title>
        <p>
            This parameter defines how serialization should work in RPC services
            and is present in both client and server configurations.
        </p>
        <p>
            The serialization process is used to encode and decode data in RPC requests,
            so that the data can be transferred through the network.
        </p>
        <p>
            Currently only <code>StringFormat</code> and <code>BinaryFormat</code> from
            <a href="https://github.com/Kotlin/kotlinx.serialization">kotlinx.serialization</a> are supported,
            and by default you can choose from Json, Protobuf or Cbor formats:
        </p>

        <code-block lang="kotlin">
            rpcClientConfig {
                serialization {
                    json { /* this: JsonBuilder from kotlinx.serialization */ }
                    cbor { /* this: CborBuilder from kotlinx.serialization */ }
                    protobuf { /* this: ProtobufBuilder from kotlinx.serialization */ }
                }
            }
        </code-block>
        <p>
            Only last defined format will be used to serialize requests.
            If no format is specified, a runtime error will be thrown.
            You can also define a custom format.
        </p>
    </chapter>
    <chapter id="connector-dsl">
        <title>
            <code>connector</code> DSL
        </title>
        <p>
            Connector is a part of kRPC that is responsible for sending and receiving data over the network.
            You can configure the following parameters:
        </p>
        <list>
            <li>
                <code>waitTimeout</code> - timeout for waiting for a service to be registered.
                Sometimes services can be registered after the server starts,
                and after the first requests starts to arrive from a peer.
                This parameter defines how long the server will wait for a service to be registered.
                <br/>
                The default value is <code>Duration.INFINITE</code>.
                <br/>
                Also, available a value of <code>dontWait()</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>callTimeout</code> - timeout for processing one message.
                <br/>
                The default value is <code>Duration.INFINITE</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>perCallBufferSize</code> - size of the messages buffer for one call.
                The buffer ignores the size in bytes and only counts the number of messages.
                This effectively provides a backpressure mechanism.
                If a peer is slow to process the message during a call,
                the buffer will be filled up and
                the sender will wait before sending more messages.
                <br/>
                Note that this is <b>per call</b>, not per connection.
                <br/>
                The default value is <code>1000</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>messageCompression</code> - compression for serialized kRPC messages.
                See <a anchor="message-compression"/> for details.
                <br/>
                The default value is <code>null</code> (compression is disabled).
            </li>
        </list>
        <p>
            Example:
        </p>
        <code-block lang="kotlin">
            rpcClientConfig {
                connector {
                    waitTimeout = 10.seconds
                    callTimeout = 60.seconds
                    perCallBufferSize = 1000
                }
            }
        </code-block>
        <chapter id="message-compression">
            <title>Message compression</title>
            <p>
                kRPC can compress serialized messages before sending them over the network.
                Compression is disabled by default and is used only when both endpoints
                configure a codec with the same name.
                The codec is negotiated during the connection handshake,
                so endpoints automatically fall back to uncompressed messages
                when a peer does not configure a matching codec
                (for example, when it runs an older version of the library).
            </p>
            <p>
                Zstandard compression is provided by the
                <code>kotlinx-rpc-krpc-compression-zstd</code> artifact:
            </p>
            <code-block lang="kotlin">
                dependencies {
                    implementation("org.jetbrains.kotlinx:kotlinx-rpc-krpc-compression-zstd:%kotlinx-rpc-version%")
                }
            </code-block>
            <code-block lang="kotlin">
                rpcClientConfig {
                    connector {
                        zstd()
                    }
                }
            </code-block>
            <p>
                The <code>zstd</code> function accepts the following parameters:
            </p>
            <list>
                <li>
                    <code>minimumMessageSize</code> - minimum serialized message size in bytes
                    before compression is attempted.
                    Smaller messages are sent uncompressed,
                    as are messages that compression does not make smaller.
                    <br/>
                    The default value is <code>1024</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <code>maxDecompressedMessageSize</code> - maximum decompressed message size in bytes
                    accepted from a peer.
                    The limit is advertised during the handshake,
                    and peers send larger messages uncompressed instead of exceeding it.
                    <br/>
                    The default value is <code>64 * 1024 * 1024</code> (64 MiB).
                    The Zstandard decoder separately rejects frames that require a history window
                    larger than 8 MiB.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <code>compressionLevel</code> - the Zstandard compression level.
                    <br/>
                    The default value is <code>3</code>.
                </li>
            </list>
            <p>
                Compression preserves the transport message kind:
                string messages stay string messages and binary messages stay binary messages,
                so custom <code>KrpcTransport</code> implementations that support
                only one kind keep working.
                Custom codecs can be plugged in by implementing <code>KrpcMessageCodec</code>
                and setting <code>messageCompression</code> directly.
            </p>
            <p>
                Invalid or over-limit compressed envelopes terminate the connection.
                This fails pending calls instead of leaving them suspended without a response.
                At most two compressed envelopes are decoded concurrently within a process,
                bounding aggregate decompression allocations across concurrent connections.
            </p>
        </chapter>
    </chapter>
</topic>
